{"auditVerdictAtHead":"pass","canonicalDigest":"sha256:ced17a693c1301fd313392cb2229ef439aabf2d0489e47d72f896be409270ae9","canonicalizationVersion":"1","changeRequest":{"author":"buger","body":"Fixes #14154.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The Apps menu now sorts names with `localeCompare`, the way its own search results and the shell's other lists already do. Before, it compared UTF-16 code units, so on a non-English desktop every app whose name starts with an accented letter (É, Ü, Ö, Å, Ł, …) was listed after Z.\n\n| Apps menu, `LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8` | `quattro` `a85e29a` | This PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| Calculatrice, Écrans, Éditeur de texte, Fichiers, Musique, Zed | Calculatrice, Fichiers, Musique, Zed, **Écrans, Éditeur de texte** | Calculatrice, **Écrans, Éditeur de texte**, Fichiers, Musique, Zed |\n| the same apps, `LANG=en_US.UTF-8` or `C.UTF-8` (English names) | alphabetical | unchanged |\n| `en_US.UTF-8`: 1Password, Alacritty, Zed, `~Tilde`, `_Under`, `(beta) X` | `(beta) X`, 1Password, `_Under`, Alacritty, Zed, `~Tilde` (code-unit order scatters the symbols) | `_Under`, `(beta) X`, `~Tilde`, 1Password, Alacritty, Zed (symbols first, then digits, then letters) |\n| `ru_RU.UTF-8`: Alacritty, Zed, Терминал | Alacritty, Zed, Терминал | Терминал, Alacritty, Zed (the language's own script first) |\n| two apps with the same name | ordered by id | unchanged |\n\n### What changes\n\n```diff\n--- shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml\n           var aLabel = String(a.label || \"\").toLowerCase()\n           var bLabel = String(b.label || \"\").toLowerCase()\n-          if (aLabel < bLabel) return -1\n-          if (aLabel > bLabel) return 1\n+          var byLabel = aLabel.localeCompare(bLabel)\n+          if (byLabel !== 0) return byLabel\n           var aId = String(a.itemId || \"\")\n--- shell/services/AppSearch.js\n     if (q && a.score !== b.score) return b.score - a.score\n-    if (a.key < b.key) return -1\n-    if (a.key > b.key) return 1\n-    if (a.name < b.name) return -1\n-    if (a.name > b.name) return 1\n-    return 0\n+    var byKey = a.key.localeCompare(b.key)\n+    if (byKey !== 0) return byKey\n+    var aId = String(a.entry.id || \"\")\n+    var bId = String(b.entry.id || \"\")\n+    if (aId < bId) return -1\n+    if (aId > bId) return 1\n+    return 0\n```\n\nThe names already come from each desktop entry's `Name[<lang>]` for the user's language, and on a normal desktop Qt collates in that same language, so the sort follows it. The lowercasing and the id tie-break are kept.\n\n<details><summary>Notes</summary>\n\n- `localeCompare` returns 0 only for names that collate as equal, and those still fall through to the id, so the order stays stable.\n- Under `C.UTF-8` the shell compares UTF-16 code units, exactly as `<` did, so the order there is exactly what it was before, accented names included (checked in the real shell).\n- The order follows the locale Qt collates with (`LC_ALL`, then `LC_COLLATE`, then `LANG`), as every other `localeCompare` in the shell does, while the names follow the language Quickshell picks for `Name[<lang>]`. They agree on a normal French or German desktop. On a desktop whose language has its own script, that script now comes first: with `LANG=ru_RU.UTF-8` Cyrillic names sort before the Latin ones (Greek under `el_GR`, Chinese under `zh_CN`), where `quattro` put them after Z. Under `en_US` or `fr_FR` they still come after the Latin names. I checked all of these in the real shell.\n- `AppSearch.sortedEntries`, which the plugin API hands the app list out through, compared names with `<` too. It uses `localeCompare` now, and breaks a tie between names that collate the same (a composed and a decomposed É) on the desktop id, the same order the Apps menu gets from its row ids. So a plugin that lists apps sees the same order as the menu.\n- Sorting 300 rows with the new comparator takes 1.1 ms against 1.0 ms before, in Qt 6.11's QML engine.\n\n</details>\n\nA 48-second narrated capture of the real shell under Quickshell with `LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8`. On upstream `quattro` `a85e29a`, Écrans and Éditeur de texte come after Zed. With the fix (recorded at `c5ed437`, the first commit of this branch; the second changes only the plugin list's tie-break and the test) they sort under E:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/059c3562-4a03-4c3f-b491-a6200ab7ba56\n\n### How to check it\n\n- [x] new `test/shell.d/menu-apps-order-test.sh` (7 `ok`). It takes the Apps comparator out of `Menu.qml` as written and sorts with it under node with `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, so it collates in the same root order whatever the caller's locale is; it pins the comparator, not Qt's collation (that is what the real-shell check below covers): French and German names land under their letter, case is ignored, and equal names are ordered by id. Three cases run `AppSearch.sortedEntries`: the same names, and a composed and a decomposed É with different desktop ids, fed in both orders, which must come out in id order. One more pins the new order for names that start with a symbol. It fails on `quattro` (`actual: [\"Calculatrice\",\"Fichiers\",\"Terminal\",\"Zed\",\"Éditeur de texte\",\"Übersetzer\"]`).\n- [x] mutations: going back to `<`/`>` in either file, reversing the comparison, dropping the lowercasing, dropping or reversing either id tie-break, or using the previous code-unit tie-break in `AppSearch.js` each make it fail; without the `LC_ALL` pin it fails under `et_EE` and `th_TH`.\n- [x] the test passes the same under `C`, `POSIX`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US`, `et_EE`, `th_TH`, `tr_TR`, `sv_SE` and `ru_RU`.\n- [x] the menu suites still pass: `menu-test` 127, `menu-guards` 18, `menu-plugin` 12, `menu-images` 6, `keybindings-menu` 16, `app-search` 24\n- [x] the full `./test/shell` run fails the same 18 files before and after (my Linux box isn't an Omarchy install). The new file passes.\n- [x] in the real shell (Apps summoned over IPC): with `LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8`, `quattro` lists `Calculatrice, Fichiers, Musique, Zed, Écrans, Éditeur de texte` and this branch lists `Calculatrice, Écrans, Éditeur de texte, Fichiers, Musique, Zed`. With `en_US.UTF-8` the English names keep their order, and with `C.UTF-8` both trees give the same order even for accented names. The one visible change for English names is the third row of the table: names starting with a symbol now sort ahead of digits and letters instead of being scattered by code unit. With `ru_RU`, `el_GR` and `zh_CN`, the language's own script moves ahead of the Latin names, as in the table.\n\n<details><summary>Commands</summary>\n\n```sh\nbash test/shell.d/menu-apps-order-test.sh\n\n# the same test against quattro's Menu.qml (expect: not ok - apps menu files an accented name under its letter, not after Z)\ngit checkout a85e29a -- shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml && bash test/shell.d/menu-apps-order-test.sh; git checkout HEAD -- shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### What it could affect\n\nOnly the order of the Apps menu (and of the same list as plugins get it), and only where a name starts outside a–z: accented letters (the fix); names that start with a symbol or an emoji, which now group at the top under the same collation the search results use (an emoji-led name used to come last); and, on a Russian, Greek or Chinese desktop, names in that script, which now come before the Latin ones instead of after Z. Through `AppSearch.sortedEntries`, search matches with the same score now also follow that collation, which only plugins see.\n\n---\n\n**Independent verification:** [the evidence for this PR](https://portal.reqproof.com/projects/omarchy/changes/pr/14155) comes from a full proof audit of `c4a14aa` merged onto `quattro` at `393a43d` (evaluated merge `7bfbccd`): test report 60 passed · 0 failed · 0 skipped, plus 17 tests observed by the behaviour comparison run: 17 passed.\n","bodyDigest":"sha256:ff32136405dda7b067b0ed49e38080e3c4952618b8354ab390d40cec57c96387","bodyExcerpt":"Fixes #14154.","linkedIssues":["#14154"],"source":"explici